//@ bundled
// tests/samples/stdlib/networking.fin is the specification, and it says, in full:
// "NOT IMPLEMENTED YET". So does this. The module exists because two imports in the
// corpus name it -- `importing.fin:5` takes the whole module and `:6` takes it as
// `net` -- and an import of a module that is not there is a hard error, so an empty
// module is the difference between measuring the import and measuring nothing.
//
// It lives under lib/std even though `import networking;` writes no `::std` selector.
// That is not a claim that networking is a std module: nothing checks the selector at
// all, which is booked as KnownDefect_BundledStdlib.TheNamespaceSelectorInAnImportIsNotChecked.
// When the selector starts being checked, a non-std module moves up to lib/.
//
// `importing.fin:5` says the whole-module form "allows to be used like
// networking.invoke()". There is nothing to invoke yet.
//
// IT STAYS EMPTY, DELIBERATELY, and this is a decision rather than an omission. It was
// reconsidered while the rest of lib/std was rewritten, because two things had changed: an
// `@define`d extern reached through an import does lower, and the same libc route that gave
// `stdio.fin` its `File` and `math.fin` its libm would give this module `socket`, `bind`,
// `listen`, `accept`, `connect`, `send` and `recv`. So a networking module that compiles and
// runs is reachable.
//
// It is not written because nothing says what it should look like. The draft is one line of
// prose; no sample calls a single networking name; `importing.fin` names `invoke()`, which
// is not an operation any socket API has. Every signature would therefore be invented, and
// ADR 0008 is explicit that a name declared nowhere in the tree may not be invented -- that
// ruling is about `PathLike` and `pointer_type`, and a whole module of invented names is the
// same act at a larger scale. A wrong API that compiles is worse than an empty module,
// because the empty module tells the truth and a plausible one has to be un-designed later
// by whoever finds it does not fit.
//
// What would unblock it: a sample, or an owner ruling on the surface -- blocking or
// non-blocking, whether an address is a `string` or a struct, and whether a failure is a
// `Result`, an `IOResult` or a `blame`. `stdio.fin`'s `File` is the shape it would follow if
// the answer were "the simplest thing that works": statics over libc, `blame` on failure.
// That is a guess, and it is written here as a guess rather than as code.
namespace std {
}
